  "query_preview": "..\n\n- Synoptic Setup:\n\nA low pressure system over eastern Wyoming will continue keep low\nclouds from now through tomorrow morning. A similar set up to\nyesterday is likely, with low clouds presenting as patchy fog.\nVisibility drops to 2 miles in localized areas of higher terrain \nuntil noon today, especially along HWY 200, 200S, and 253.\n\nA ridge is expected to build in Tuesday through Friday bringing\nsouthwesterly upper level flow and warmer temperatures. By midweek\na shortwave trough will move through the region, remaining mostly\naffecting the southeast part of the area, bringing rain showers \nand possibly a thunderstorm or two.\n\nThursday and Friday will see widespread 80 degree afternoon \ntemperatures. Highest temperatures of the week trend warmer on \nThursday now, suggesting a quicker exit. Towards the end of the \nweek an upper level trough deepening over the PNW and pushing east\nover the Rockies will push temperatures down closer to normal as \nwell as the next likely chance for a more widespread precipitation.\nDepending on the strength of the low, wind speeds behind the cold\nfront will need to be monitored.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nMinor tweaks to cloud base and visibility grids within the first 6\nhours. No changes to NBM otherwise.\n\nWinds on Friday night through Saturday will need to be monitored,\nas we both think NBM/GFS/ECMWF are washing higher speeds out. \nStrong frontogenesis from sinking air on the cold side of the low \nis trending stronger, meaning this event will need to be watched \nfor wind headlines.",
